WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.002 --> 00:00:14.003
SMOKE TEST · SOLUTION
Short, diode, then fuse
Diagnosis (see "How to approach"): a solder blob on the bottom shorts the rail to ground, D1 is reversed, F1 is blown.
Fix the cause, prove it, and fit the fuse last.
Tools: Desolder wick (4) · Multimeter (2) · Rotate (5) · Soldering iron (3)

2
00:00:16.630 --> 00:00:23.099
STEP 1 · LOCATE
A blob of solder bridges the rail via and the ground trace.

3
00:00:23.101 --> 00:00:32.767
STEP 2 · REMOVE THE SHORT
Desolder wick (4): soak up the blob.

4
00:00:32.768 --> 00:00:46.312
STEP 3 · PROVE IT
Power off, rail to ground: OL. No more short.
Why: What is left is R1 and the LED, which only conduct once the LED's forward voltage is reached: a meter sees a high resistance.

5
00:00:46.313 --> 00:00:52.811
STEP 4 · FIX POLARITY
Rotate tool (5): turn D1 so its band matches the silkscreen.

6
00:00:52.812 --> 00:01:01.050
STEP 5 · NEW FUSE
Only now, soldering iron (3) on F1: a fresh 0.5 A fuse.

7
00:01:01.051 --> 00:01:12.024
STEP 6 · CHECK
Power on: TP1 reads +4.293 V, and D2 lights.
Why: Not 5 V: the series diode drops about 0.6–0.7 V. That drop is the price of reverse-polarity protection.

8
00:01:12.026 --> 00:01:27.492
STEP 7 · VERIFY
Run the test bench (Run tests).
Why: It powers the board, checks every objective, and only decrypts the flag if the circuit now matches the original design: a fix that merely works around a fault does not count.

9
00:01:27.493 --> 00:01:31.096
STEP 7 · VERIFY
All objectives pass and the flag is captured. Earn your own: it is blurred here.

10
00:01:31.098 --> 00:01:42.069
WHAT YOU LEARNED
Cause first, fuse last
Remove the short, prove it with Ω, fix the polarity, and only then replace the fuse.
A rail behind a series diode sits about 0.7 V below the input.
